  "bridge": "You caught yourself in the mirror this morning, pulling your skin tight, counting the lines that weren't there last year. So you booked the appointment. A vial. A promise. Younger by Friday. Two thousand years ago Rome was doing this. Rich men drank gold, bathed in wine, paid for potions to freeze time. One of them was a philosopher named Seneca, richer than most. Then a friend asked him a question that ruined the whole game. How many more years do you want? The friend said more. Always more. This man owned villas, slaves, every pleasure coin can buy. And he still woke up terrified of the mirror. He counted his lines. He was never satisfied. Seneca saw the trap. The chase had no finish line. The problem was never the wrinkles. It was a man who could never feel full. Seneca knew what people like that, and like the you this morning, kept forgetting.",
